## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Practices and concept areas | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Programs with memory. *How does a program remember, change and reuse what it knows, and how do we build one as a team?* Anchor: a short rain-gauge program shown in blocks and in the text view, which keeps a running total of a week of readings and says when a day was the wettest so far | 16 | g6-variable-types; g6-procedures; g6-inclusive-collab | MS-PRO-VD-16; MS-PRO-PD-12; MS-PRO-PD-15 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT7 (a procedure as a reusable part); CT8 (plan the program before building it); CT9 (test with a test-table, change one thing, retest); IC4 (a team agreement, shared goals and checkpoints); IC5 (driver and navigator roles that switch on the timer); IC3 (written feedback to another team) | Session 1 (unplugged): be the computer on a paper program in both views; a variable is a named box that holds one value of one type at a time: a number, a string of text, a true or false value (Boolean); assignment read "becomes". Session 2 (device): our editor shows blocks and the text view side by side; a worked program is traced, then run; values stored and read back. Session 3 (device, modify): change and combine values (add to a number, join two strings, flip a Boolean); a typed "5" and the number 5 are not the same, shown by a quick error, and one value converted to another type. Session 4 (trace): a choice that reads a variable (if the total is over 20, if raining is true). Session 5 (trace): a count-controlled repeat that updates a variable each time round, traced row by row. Session 6 (device, modify): the anchor modified to count rainy days, track the largest and smallest reading and count down a timer (increment, decrement, running total, maximum and minimum, counting). Session 7 (device, make): pairs build a short program to a brief (a plant-watering log or a game score with a bonus rule) with three look-fors and a three-input test-table as review. Session 8 (trace, paper): a program with the same four lines written three times beside the version with one named procedure and three calls; a bad name and a good name. Session 9 (device, modify): fill the blanks in a procedure version, then replace repeated code in a second program with calls. Session 10 (device, make): find the repeated or grouped steps in a given program, make a procedure, call it, and write two lines on how it helps (less repeated code, one place to update). Session 11 (unplugged): the team agreement (every idea heard, everyone types, roles switch), feedback frames, and the team's plan on paper with each member's idea recorded. Sessions 12 to 14 (device, make): the team program to a brief (a class garden helper with variables of two types, a choice, a repeat and a procedure), drafted, given written feedback by another team with the checklist, revised and retested; the team writes who spoke, who typed and what they changed. Out: no lists or other collections (grade 7's g7-lists), no procedures with parameters or return values (high school's g9-optimize-procedures), no professional syntax beyond what the editor shows, no formal team methods. End-of-unit (day 15): unseen items: trace a program with a number, a string and a Boolean; predict a choice on a variable; finish a running-total trace; spot a type error; rewrite a repeated block as a procedure on paper; a short build or debug task in the editor (paper version); the team program is scored from its revised version (session 14) against the rubric, with the reflection; respond day 16. Tools: our own editor (blocks and text view side by side, step-through trace, stated tests with "yes" or "not yet" per look-for; job: build, trace and test every program; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the anchor and every worked, blank-filled and buggy program in both views with tested solutions, trace-table sheets, the team brief, rubric and feedback checklist | First because every later device day runs in this editor and reads the text view, and the progression orders g6-variable-types (it introduces the text-program) before g6-procedures and g6-inclusive-collab, which use it, and g6-procedures before unit 2's g6-model-algorithm, which calls a procedure. The team program closes the unit because it needs variables, a choice, a repeat and a procedure together. Weeks 1 to 8. Base: archived Year 7 block-language programming part I, lessons 1 to 6, and part II, lessons 7 to 9 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's operators lesson as the comparison and joining steps inside session 3, zero added days | Pairs are assigned, not chosen, and roles switch on the timer so no student holds the keyboard all period; feedback names the program, never the person. The brief's data are about things (rain, plants, a game), never a student. Devices: cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 2 | Models of an algorithm. *How can we show what an algorithm does, prove it works, and see the same thinking in people's jobs?* Anchor: a library book-return routine written as a paragraph of words, which the class must turn into something a new helper could follow without asking | 11 | g6-model-algorithm; g6-verify-algorithm; g6-careers-ct | MS-ALG-PS-02; MS-ALG-PS-03; MS-SOC-CE-44 | Concept area: algorithms and design, with computing and society (careers). Practices: CT7 (the same algorithm in two models); CT8 (follow a template before modeling alone); CT9 (test inputs that might break it); HCD11 (refine the algorithm when a test fails); CT6 and IC4 (how workers break a problem into parts) | Session 1 (unplugged): the anchor's words turned into a flowchart with a loop (for each book), a choice (late or not) and a call to a named procedure (shelve the book), using unit 1's procedures; the three control structures named. Session 2: pseudocode introduced, built from the flowchart line by line, indented under each loop and choice. Session 3 (modify): a model with one wrong step against its words; trace one input through both models and fix the model that disagrees. Session 4 (make): model a new given algorithm (a recycling sort or a school bell schedule) both ways with the class template. Session 5 (unplugged): verify: choose test inputs (a typical case, the smallest, the largest, one that should fail), fill a test-table of expected against traced results. Session 6 (device): a given sorting program and a shape-drawing program in the editor run on the chosen inputs; a mismatch sends the student back to the algorithm, not the code. Session 7 (make): verify a given algorithm with a hidden fault and find the input that exposes it. Session 8: workers in four careers (a nurse planning medicine rounds, a farmer planning water by field, a builder planning a frame, a musician arranging a repeated chorus) and the problem each solves; students find the abstraction, the decomposition and the pattern in each, and the parallel with how they planned unit 1's programs. Session 9 (make): each student picks one worker case and writes its steps as pseudocode lines. Out: no designing an algorithm from nothing (grade 7's g7-design-with-variables), no efficiency, proof or comparison of algorithms, no converting pseudocode into running code, no professional notation. End-of-unit (day 10): model an unseen algorithm as a flowchart and as pseudocode (one loop, one choice, one procedure call); build a test-table for an unseen algorithm and say whether it is accurate; analyze a new worker case for two computational thinking moves; respond day 11. Tools: our own editor (job: run the given programs on the students' test inputs, session 6). Curio Class writes: the anchor routine and the new algorithms in words, the flowchart and pseudocode templates, the test-table sheets, the four worker cases drawn from the Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook (bls.gov/ooh, job duties as of the scope's date) | After unit 1 because the flowchart calls a named procedure, which g6-procedures teaches first, and pseudocode is read beside unit 1's text view. Verify follows model because the test-table is filled against a model. Careers comes last because g6-careers-ct writes pseudocode lines, which g6-model-algorithm introduces. Weeks 9 to 14. Our own (the partial base's Year 7 has no flowchart or careers unit) | Candidate: kept, the verify sessions' sorting and shape examples as the boundary's own kinds of algorithm, zero added days | Worker cases show many kinds of work, rural and town, without ranking them; no student is asked about a family member's job, and a student's own offer is welcome. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 3 | Data with care. *What can a table tell us, what does it hide, and what do we do when it is messy?* Anchor: two weather logs of the same school week, one recorded each hour to the tenth of a degree and one once a day to the whole degree, and one question only one of them can answer | 10 | g6-precision-metadata; g6-sort-filter-tool; g6-data-quality; g6-data-about-people | MS-DAT-DC-21; MS-DAT-DC-22; MS-DAT-DC-23; MS-DAT-DC-24 | Concept area: data and analysis. Practices: CT7 (which detail a question needs); IC3 (say why each tool step helps the question); CT9 and HCD11 (try a cleaning option, check what it did); ESR2 (data about people kept invented and private) | Session 1 (unplugged): precision (how exact a value is) and granularity (how often or how finely it is recorded), with the anchor; what each log can answer, what it costs to store (cells counted), and when more matters (a hospital monitor against a casual step count, told as a case, no student data) (MS-DAT-DC-21). Session 2 (unplugged): metadata is data about data (who, when, where, what unit); a table with and without its header; a question that cannot be answered without the metadata (MS-DAT-DC-22). Session 3 (device): the properties of a supplied photo of a school plant, where the time and place were added without anyone typing them; metadata can be collected without our knowing. Session 4 (unplugged): a first look at what a record about a person holds beyond what they typed, on invented profile cards; who might want it and for what, one benefit and one risk on a benefit-risk-chart; MS-DAT-IM-28 is begun here and owned in grade 7 (g7-personal-data). Session 5 (device, worked): a supplied structured table of school garden and weather records in the table tool, sorted and filtered in a worked example. Session 6 (device, modify): group and summarize: count, total, smallest, largest and average (the total shared out evenly), each step explained in an "I am ___ so that ___" sentence. Session 7 (device, make): pairs answer three questions of the table and make one summary chart (MS-DAT-DC-23). Session 8 (unplugged): a messy table (a missing value, a duplicate row, an impossible number, two spellings of one thing, a date in the wrong form) and the cause of each (a skipped reading, a typing slip, a faulty sensor). Session 9 (unplugged): the options for each problem (remove the row, fill with a typical value, reformat, ask again, leave as is), what each does to the answer, and how removing rows can leave one group out (MS-DAT-DC-24). Session 10 (device or paper, make): two options tried on a copy of the table and the answers compared; no option is marked the correct one. Out: no collecting data at several precisions (shown by the teacher), no metadata formats or hand-editing metadata, no cleaning in the tool-use sessions, no large data sets, no automatic error finders, no real personal data. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 38) stands for it. Tools: a table tool in the page (a spreadsheet-style grid that sorts, filters, groups and summarizes; job: answer questions of a structured table; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md); file properties on the school device or a printed panel. Curio Class writes: the two weather logs, the garden and weather table (invented for practice), the photo and its printed properties panel, the invented profile cards, the messy table and its option cards | After unit 2 because the table tool's steps are read as an algorithm (sort, then filter, then group) and test-table habits carry into checking a cleaning option. Before unit 4 because the progression puts all four data rows before grade 7's training-data row, and unit 4 shows its classifier's training set as a table. Its sort and summarize sessions fall in weeks 16 and 17, before math grade 6 unit 8 (see Cross-subject). Weeks 14 to 19. Base: archived Year 7 spreadsheets unit, lessons 1, 2 and 4 for sessions 5 to 7 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's quick-calculations lesson as session 6's summaries, zero added days | Profile cards are invented and say so; no student enters or is asked for their own data, devices or accounts. The hospital monitor case is a told example. Session 4's teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. Agent reads: sensitivity (the FTC privacy guidance in the starting pack). |
| 4 | Learning machines and who they serve. *How does a machine decide, and who does a design work for?* Anchor: a sorter in the page that labels every striped leaf picture "oak", and a class that has to work out why | 7 | g6-ml-hypothesis; g6-hcd-evaluate | MS-ALG-ML-06; MS-ALG-IM-09 | Concept area: algorithms and design (machine learning; impacts of algorithms and design). Practices: CT7 (the pattern a model seems to use); CT9 (test the hypothesis, revise it); HCD10 (users who meet a design differently); ESR1 (who a design helps and who it leaves out) | Session 1 (unplugged): the teacher sorts picture cards by a hidden rule; students watch inputs and outputs, write a hypothesis for the rule and test it with cards they choose. Session 2 (device): a small trained classifier in the page with its training set shown as a table; students note its outputs on given pictures and write a hypothesis about which features it uses. Session 3 (device, modify): each pair chooses new examples to test its hypothesis, records results in a test-table and revises the hypothesis; the model is a rule learned from data, not a mind (MS-ALG-ML-06). Session 4 (unplugged): human-centered design principles as a checklist: empathy, accessibility, user needs, fairness, sustainability, accountability; how a multilingual user, a user with a disability and a user with slow or no home internet would meet the same technology. Session 5 (make): pairs evaluate an existing technology the class knows from printed screens (the school library catalog page or a bus timetable page, made generic) and the session 2 sorter, marking each principle present or missing with evidence and suggesting one improvement (MS-ALG-IM-09). Out: no explaining how a model works inside, no training or improving a model (grade 7's g7-improve-training-data), no formal user research, usability testing or accessibility standards, no designing a full technology. End-of-unit (day 6): from an unseen classifier's outputs, write a hypothesis and the test that would check it; from unseen screens of a technology, mark two principles present or missing with evidence and name the user most affected; respond day 7. Tools: a toy classifier in the page (picture inputs, its training set visible, labels out; job: give outputs to reason from). Curio Class writes: the hidden-rule card set, the leaf and shape pictures and the training set (invented for practice), the printed generic screens | After unit 3 because the training set is read as a data table and its quality is unit 3's question; before grade 7, which assumes g6-ml-hypothesis for both g7-pick-approach and g7-improve-training-data. The design audit sits with the classifier so the sorter's failures become evidence of a design missing users. Weeks 20 to 23. Our own (the partial base's Year 7 has no machine learning unit) | Candidate: kept, a second sorter whose training set has few examples of one kind, as an extension on session 3, zero added days | Disability and language: talk is about what a design lets a person do, never about a classmate; use the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. No faces, voices or pictures of people go into the classifier. Some homes have slow or no internet: no student is asked about home access. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 5 | Packets and protection. *How does a message cross a network in pieces, and what keeps the information safe?* Anchor: a forty-word message that must cross the room, but each runner may carry only five words | 7 | g6-packets; g6-cia-practice | MS-SYS-NT-34 | Concept area: systems and security (networks; security). Practices: CT6 (what each packet must carry to arrive); CT7 (the packet-strip as a model of the trip); IC3 (explain the trip to a partner) | Session 1 (unplugged): the anchor message cut into numbered packet cards, each with a header (to, from, number) and its part of the message in ones and zeros from a supplied code card; students act as routers and send packets by different routes on the taped room network-diagram; the receiver rebuilds the message by number. Session 2 (unplugged): a packet is lost and the receiver asks again; causes of loss (a crowded link, interference, a broken part) and ways to lessen it (send again, take another route); the packet-strip drawn. Session 3 (device, one screen allowed): a toy network in the page sends a message, shows packets taking different routes and drops one when a link is crowded. Session 4 (unplugged): three short cases sorted to the side of the cia-triangle each hurts (a grade list sent to the wrong people, a score changed on the way, a school site that will not load); MS-SYS-SE-32 is begun here and owned in grade 7 (g7-cia-effects). Session 5 (make): pairs model a new message's trip for a given scenario with a loss, on a packet-strip and a network-diagram, and explain what the numbers and the resend are for and which side of the triangle a lost packet hurts. Out: no protocol names or detail, no configuring real routers or other hardware, no conversion of numbers to binary by hand (the code card does it), no effects of ignoring the triangle (grade 7), no scanning or testing of a real network. End-of-unit (day 6): model an unseen scenario's packets from send to rebuild, name a cause of a loss shown and one way to lessen it, sort one new case to the triangle; respond day 7. Tools: a toy network in the page (nodes, links and packets, a crowded-link switch; job: watch packets route and drop). Curio Class writes: the packet cards and code card, the room network-diagram, the triangle cases, the scenario sheets | After unit 4 so the year's two security and networking rows sit together and run in weeks 24 to 27, beside science grade 6 unit 5's digital signals (see Cross-subject); the progression puts g6-packets before grade 7's g7-internet-resilience. The triangle practice follows packets because a lost packet is an availability case. Base: archived Year 7 networks unit, lessons 3 to 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's performance lesson as session 2's loss causes, zero added days | Walking routes: clear paths, no running, cords away; a student who cannot walk the route is a fixed router. Security cases use toy systems and invented schools, never a real school or home network. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 6 | Systems, history and choices. *Who shapes computing, and what follows from the choices people make with it?* Anchor: two systems that do the same job, a library kiosk and a home tablet, and a family and a library that each need one | 9 | g6-compare-systems; g6-devices-industry; g6-computing-eras; g6-human-decisions | MS-SYS-HW-30; MS-SYS-HW-31; MS-SOC-HI-38; MS-SOC-HU-43 | Concept area: systems and security (hardware and software), with computing and society (history; humans and computing). Practices: HCD10 (users with different needs); HCD12 (who is kept informed and in control of a system or a choice); CT6 (break a device's job into input, processing, storage, output); IC3 (explain a comparison); ESR1 and ESR2 (who gains and who is harmed by a choice; respect for others' data) | Session 1 (unplugged): the anchor's two systems compared on a system-diagram by features and requirements (storage, connectivity, accessibility features) and user needs (portability, cost, battery life), with one societal, environmental or ethical impact (the cost gap between homes, energy use and discarded devices, privacy on a shared kiosk). Session 2 (make): groups choose a system for an invented user and justify it, naming an impact (MS-SYS-HW-30). Session 3: computing devices in industries (a farm soil sensor, a factory arm, a hospital monitor, a store checkout, a car's control unit) on system-diagrams with input, processing, storage, output and any mechanical part; each student chooses an industry and a device from the set to study. Session 4 (one screen or cards, make): a toy greenhouse sensor, controller and fan loop in the page; students describe their chosen device's function and the task or problem it serves (MS-SYS-HW-31). Session 5 (unplugged): three eras and today on a timeline (counting machines, room-sized computers, personal and networked computers, computing now), with who shaped each by designing, funding, regulating and using it: an individual, a community, an organization, a government; their motivations and how they influenced one another. Picks: the 1890 US census tabulation (census.gov history pages), the mathematicians who computed by hand for NASA's predecessor and then programmed its first electronic computers (nasa.gov biographies), the network a US defense research agency funded (darpa.mil), and today's public library computer access (imls.gov reports). Session 6 (make): pairs compare the roles of two kinds of actor across two eras from the short source pack, without ranking them (MS-SOC-HI-38). Session 7 (unplugged): case cards of people's choices in using computing (sharing a private photo of a friend, copying a classmate's code, reporting a bug) and in designing it (what data a school app collects, which rule a sorting system uses, how a result is read); the ethical and social consequences for fairness, equity, privacy and well-being, with two views and a dated source per real case. Session 8: decisions, not the technology alone, shape each outcome; students say who decided and who was affected. Session 9 (make): a short written claim on one case with two views and a more responsible choice proposed (MS-SOC-HU-43). Out: no technical specifications (clock speeds, architecture), no building or programming devices, no memorized dates or ranking of people, no deep ethical analysis or positions on whether machines think, no advanced audits. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 63) stands for it. Tools: a toy sensor and controller loop in the page (job: show input, processing and output in one device). Curio Class writes: the system cards and user profiles (invented for practice), the industry device cards, the era timeline and source pack with dated teacher source lines, the decision case cards (invented for practice where no real case is named) | Last because it gathers the year: unit 1's programs, unit 3's data choices, unit 4's design principles and unit 5's networks reappear as systems people choose, shaped and used. Its rows need nothing taught after them, and the year-end check in the finals week stands for its end-of-unit assessment. Weeks 27 to 31. Our own (the partial base's Year 7 has no systems or society unit) | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a local worker who uses an industry device; the cards carry the row and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | Companies are never named; government bodies and courts may be, with sources in the teacher source line only (STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days"). The mathematicians' story names the segregation they worked under as a fact with its source, keeping the talk on their work and its credit. The photo case: no student says whose photo or what happened to them; sessions 7 to 9's teacher pages carry health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. No student is asked about the devices or internet at home. Agent reads: sensitivity (the NASA pages and the FTC guidance in the starting pack). |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
