| 1 | Read it, then design it. *What does each line of a program do, and how do we plan one that keeps track of things?* Anchor: a twenty-line quiz program (invented for practice) that stores a player's name, keeps a number score, sets a true or false "passed" value, asks three questions in a loop and chooses a message, printed and traced before it is ever run | 10 | g7-analyze-code; g7-design-with-variables | MS-PRO-RD-17; MS-ALG-PS-01 | Concept area: programming, with algorithms and design. Practices: IC3 (explain what each line does); CT7 (spot the parts that repeat and the parts that choose); CT6 (define the problem before designing); IC5 (driver and navigator roles that switch at the halfway signal) | Session 1 (unplugged): the anchor printed; after the hook, a retrieval trace of grade 6's variable types; students "be the computer" with a trace-table and predict the output for two players; the notional machine: one line at a time, in order, exactly as written. Session 2 (device): run it and compare; a margin note on the role of each variable (a number that grows, text that is stored, a true or false value that is set and checked). Session 3: the roles of the loop (what repeats, how many times) and the choice (which branch runs, and why only one); predict for two inputs before running. Session 4: a version with two procedures, each called more than once, no parameters; the role of each procedure; retrieval of grade 6's procedures. Session 5 (modify, device): change the anchor in small checked steps (add a question, move the pass mark) and retrace. Session 6 (design, unplugged): design an algorithm for a new problem with variables of three types (a unit converter from a given rate table, or a reading-log tracker that keeps a name, a page count and a "goal met" value) as a flowchart and pseudocode; trace it with sample inputs to show each variable created, updated and used. Session 7 (make, device): build the design in text, blocks as a stand-in; a partner traces it. Session 8: revise the design after the partner's trace and write how it works. Out: procedures with parameters or return values (grade 9), debugging and testing as scored skills (unit 2's MS-PRO-TR-19), lists and other data structures (MS-ALG-PS-01 does not expect them; lists come in unit 2), and no score on whether the built program runs: MS-ALG-PS-01 is met by the design and its trace. End-of-unit (day 9): mark the roles of loop, choice, variables and procedures in a new twenty-line program and predict its output for two inputs (MS-PRO-RD-17); design a short algorithm for a new problem with a number, a text and a true or false variable, as pseudocode with a trace-table (MS-ALG-PS-01); respond day 10. Tools: our own editor (text view, blocks view, step-through trace; job: run and step the anchor and the student's build). Curio Class writes: the anchor quiz and its two-procedure version, the trace-table and design sheets, the converter rate table, two sample answers for each end-of-unit task | First because every later program, and grade 8's project, needs the notional machine and the roles of each construct, and MS-PRO-RD-17 comes after grade 6's procedures (STANDARD.md ordering: MS-PRO-PD-12 before RD-17; VD-16 before PS-01 and RD-17). Reading comes before designing (APPROACH.md order), so the student designs with constructs already traced. Base: archived Year 8 "Introduction to Python programming", all six lessons (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's order (input and output, then arithmetic, then selection, then iteration) inside sessions 1 to 3, zero added days | No student's real name or detail goes into a program: the quiz stores a made-up player name. "x = x + 1" is read as "x becomes x plus one", never as an equation, with a teacher note. Devices stay on the table, cords behind it. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
