 list, a team roster of made-up names, a recycling tally) with its test-table and change log; retrieval of grade 6's team agreement. Session 19 (code review): pairs swap programs and review with a kind, specific checklist (what works, one thing unclear, one test to add), and one note on the program's prompts and messages (clear words, readable without color); peer review is MS-PRO-PD-15 used again, owned in grade 6. Session 20: act on the review, refine the prompts and messages, retest and log. Session 21: final retest and hand-in of program, test-table and change log, scored against stated look-fors (draft 17 and 18, written feedback 19, revision 20). Out: version control, unit-testing frameworks and debugging software (not expected for MS-PRO-TR-19), professional documentation, list methods past the reference page task, a live generated-text tool. End-of-unit (day 22): trace a new list program; use a new reference page entry to write a call by hand (MS-PRO-PD-13); plan tests for a new short program, find the first failing test and log one fix with its recheck (MS-PRO-TR-19); respond day 23. Tools: our own editor (step-through with a variable watch; job: run, watch and test), the offline reference page and error guide in the page. Curio Class writes: the shelf tracker and its bug versions, the list cards, the reference page entries and error guide, the program menu, the review checklist, the test-table and change-log sheets | After unit 1 because lists, tests and reviews all read code by roles first. The reference page needs grade 6's procedures and unit 1's function calls. Testing sits with the team build so the code review is part of refining it, as MS-PRO-TR-19 asks. Across weeks 6 to 12, beside science grade 7's design-and-test unit (see Cross-subject). Base: archived Year 9 "Python programming with sequences of data" lessons 2 to 4 for sessions 11 to 13 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's pair programming and live coding on sessions 12 and 13, zero added days | Made-up names only in rosters; no student's own reading or scores go into a program. Code review is about the program, never the person: the checklist's wording is kind and specific, and the teacher models one review. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
