ed source each. Session 30 (society): a short claim with two views for a new case. Session 31: each pair makes a one-page brief: one chart, its fairer redesign, and the data it would be fair to collect for it. Out: named laws and legal frameworks, encryption and secure protocols (not expected for MS-DAT-IM-28), conclusions about a population from a sample (math grade 7 unit 8; grade 8's MS-DAT-DI-25), any real person's data. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 32) stands for it. Tools: a table tool in the page (group, summarize, chart; job: the same moves on fifty rows). Curio Class writes: the invented fifty-row log, the three-chart sets, the app profile, the consent screen, the sovereignty case with its two views and sources | After the programming units so the year's first half ends on data the class can make sense of; before unit 4 because the training set there is a data-table and its bias is a data choice. Its check is mid-year, week 16. Our own: the archived Year 8 has no data unit (its Year 9 data science unit is offered to the grade 8 frame) | Candidate: kept, a chart-reading partner test on session 26, zero added days | Class data is about things (weather), never people. The profile is invented; no student says what apps they use or what is collected about them. Data sovereignty: the case is told from a nation's own published account, not as a problem to solve; use the sensitivity read's wording. The teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
