| 4 | Data that trains a model. *Can we teach a computer to tell our shapes apart, and what should we worry about when tools learn from data?* Anchor: a model trained on the class's first drawings calls every small triangle a circle | 5 | g5-collect-tools; g5-variability; g5-train-model; g5-data-benefits-risks | E5-DAT-DC-09; E5-DAT-DI-10; E5-ALG-ML-02; E5-DAT-IM-11 | Concept area: data and analysis, with machine learning (algorithms and design). Practices: CT8 (plan the training set, then train); CT9 (test the model on new examples and improve the set); CT7 (patterns and outliers in a dataset); IC3 (explain the variability); ESR1 (who gains and who is at risk); ESR2 (protect the people a dataset is about) | Session 1 (device): students collect data of two kinds with a tool, a pretend class survey on a form (which of four indoor games, how many paper-airplane loops) and the classroom thermometer's readings, then organize them in a data-table, label each column numeric or descriptive, sort a numeric column from highest to lowest and group the descriptive answers in the tool. Session 2 (unplugged and device): a given dataset of repeated toy-car ramp times (invented for practice) and the class's own three trials: values are not all the same; students mark the typical values and the outlier on a dot chart, give possible sources (starting the timer late, the car bumping the side, a recording slip, a real change in the ramp) and name one relationship between two columns (ramp height and time). Session 3 (device, make): pairs build a labeled training-set of shapes drawn on the tool's drawing pad (circles, triangles, stars), train a small classifier and test it on new drawings kept in a separate test set, counting how many of ten it got right. Session 4 (device, modify): which drawings are missing from the training-set (small, tilted, thin-lined shapes), add more varied examples, retrain and compare the counts; the class says why training data changes what a model gets right. Session 5 (society): case cards of everyday technologies that use collected data (a weather forecast, a route planner, a step-count tracker, a recommendation list); students fill a benefit-risk-chart (convenience, better predictions; privacy, wrong results, bias), name the cia-triangle side at risk, as review, and give one limit that would make it safer. Out: no mean, median or other statistic computed (math grade 6), no percents (accuracy is a count out of ten), no math of how training works, no making a data tool from scratch, no chart required of the collected data (E5-DAT-DC-09), no technical detail of how a case technology works. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 25) stands for it. Tools: a form and table tool (job: collect and organize, sort and group); a model-training tool with a drawing pad that keeps everything on the device (job: train and test; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the pretend survey, the ramp dataset (invented for practice), the dot chart, the drawing prompts, the pretend-model card set for the paper path, the four case cards with dated source lines | After unit 3 because the table and the model are read with the trace and test habits of unit 3 (the test-table becomes the model's test counts). Collecting and spreading data comes before training because a training-set is data collected and organized; the benefits and risks come last because students have just made a model that was wrong for some shapes. Base: archived Year 6 "Introduction to spreadsheets" lessons 1 and 2 for session 1; sessions 2 to 5 our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's collecting-then-formatting order on session 1, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no prompt asks about a student's home, body, food, pets or family; the survey is pretend and names no one. No camera or microphone: the model learns from drawings, never faces or voices. Case cards name no company. Ramp: a book-and-board slope on the floor, cars