| 5 | Who computing includes, and who decides. *Who were computing tools built for, and when should a person, not a computer, decide?* Anchor: a sign-up form on screen that will not accept a classmate's real-sounding invented name because it has a space and an accent mark | 6 | g5-who-included; g5-use-or-not; g5-professionals | E5-SOC-HI-16; E5-SYS-IM-15; E5-SOC-ET-17; E5-SOC-HU-18; E5-SOC-CE-19 | Concept area: computing and society (with systems and security, how systems change culture and work). Practices: ESR1 (who gains, who is left out); HCD10 (design with the people who use a tool); HCD12 (people kept informed and in control); CT6 (does this problem need computing at all?); IC3 (two views, one dated source each) | Session 1: cases where leaving a group out shaped a tool (a form that does not fit every name, a voice tool that does not hear every accent) and cases where including a group shaped it (captions made with Deaf users, a keyboard for a language made with its speakers, an early programming language written in English-like words so people who were not mathematicians could program), told by what happened with no company named, two views and a dated source each (STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days"); students place them on the timeline and list who was included or left out, what it changed, and one change they would make. Session 2: how computing systems change culture and the ways people live and work, helping and hindering (how sharing short videos changed how families keep in touch and how news spreads; how a job such as a translator's or an illustrator's is changing as generative tools arrive, from a dated labor source), without students using those tools. Session 3: when to use computing, from case cards (counting votes in a class election, translating a short sign, choosing who gets the last turn, comforting a friend), with a benefit-risk-chart and one question, should a person decide this?; then why a family might choose to use or not use a new home device (cost, privacy, who in the family can use it), without saying whether a choice is right. Session 4: how professionals in different fields work together with shared digital tools (a wildfire team, firefighters, weather scientists and mapmakers on one live map; a museum exhibit team, an artist, a historian and a programmer on one shared plan), then a short role-play of a class tool's team (designer, programmer, tester, user): what each adds and what goes wrong when one is missing. Out: no independent historical research, no dates or names beyond a major milestone's own, no ethical frameworks, laws or error rates, no use of social media or generative tools, no judgment of a family's choice. End-of-unit (day 31): for a new case, say who was included or left out and what it changed (E5-SOC-HI-16); one way a new system helps and one way it hinders how people live or work (E5-SYS-IM-15); two things a new family weighs about a new device (E5-SOC-ET-17); decide and justify whether a new problem needs computing (E5-SOC-HU-18); how two new professionals share their work with a digital tool (E5-SOC-CE-19); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: unit 2's design for users, unit 3's teams and unit 4's model that was wrong for some shapes become the evidence for who a tool includes and who should decide. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 6, so it can follow the year-end check. Base: archived Year 6 "Communication and collaboration" lessons 3 to 5 for sessions 2 and 4; sessions 1 and 3 our own | Candidate: dropped, an interview with a school worker who uses shared digital tools; case cards carry the id and a visit needs the school's own arrangement, zero days | Session 1 has disability, language and naming content: names on cards are invented, no student's own name is tested on the form, and the language keyboard case is replaced if its community's own account cannot be found (Sensitivity reads). Session 2 touches work and auto