## Cross-subject

Both leans are to core courses of grade 5 in the same year, and both core units come later in the year than the
computer science session that uses them, so each session teaches what it needs itself. Math and science grade 5 meet
every school day; this course's day n falls in week n.

### Mathematics, grade 5

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest math unit that teaches it | Bridge if math teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g5-visual-algorithm | m5-two-patterns (5.OA.B.3: two numerical patterns from two rules, in a table) | Unit 2, sessions 1 and 2 (weeks 6 and 7) | math grade 5 unit 10 (days 135 to 149, weeks 27 to 30) | Math comes later. The flowchart's counter adds the same amount each time round (by 1, by 2, by 5), counting on that grades 2 and 3 math have taught; the trace-table lists the counter's value each time round, and no second pattern, ordered pair or graph is asked, so the session stands without the math unit |

The year-end check and unit 4 use counts out of ten and whole-number comparison only; no mean (math grade 6) and no
percent (math grade 6) is used (lesson 13).

### Science, grade 5

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest science unit that teaches it | Bridge if science teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g5-variability | s5-design-test (3-5-ETS1-3: fair tests with several trials) | Unit 4, session 2 (week 21) | science grade 5 unit 6 (days 100 to 117, weeks 25 to 30) | Science comes later. The session teaches what it needs itself: why the same trial run several times gives different times, using a given dataset and three class trials, and names the sources of difference; it does not plan a fair test or change a variable, which science unit 6 does, and science can then reuse the ramp dataset idea as its own retrieval |

No row leans on English language arts or social studies. Writing a project note, explaining a strategy and weighing
two views of a case are practiced here, not owned.

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks (calendar pass).** This course's checks sit in weeks 18 and 25, off the grade 5 core
   check weeks 10, 11, 16, 17, 21, 22 and 29 to 32. docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows grade 5 week 18 already holding the
   mid-year checks of dance, media arts, theater, visual arts, physical education and ELD, and week 25 media arts' year-end
   check. They stay for the lead's one calendar pass, under the pick in the kindergarten frame's
   For the lead 1. If the pass needs week 18 freed, week 19 or 20 works by moving one unit 3 session ahead of the
   check, and the frame says which; weeks 12 to 15 would need the check after unit 2 and a new unit split.
2. **Year 6 of the partial base.** The pilot maps Year 1 to kindergarten, so Year 6 is mapped here to grade 5. The
   Year 6 lessons were placed from their titles and unit overviews on the archived pages (2026-11-25), not from the
   lesson files; the placement is checked when the store folder is made (pilot For the lead 4). Offered on:
   "Communication and collaboration" lessons 1 and 2 (internet addresses, data packets) to the grade 6 frame
   (g6-packets, MS-SYS-NT-34). The "Sensing movement" unit needs a microcontroller board (STANDARD.md section 5,
   optional); only its step-counter design idea is used, on paper.
3. **The block editor's needs for grade 5 (tool asks).** Our own editor starts with grades 3 to 5 (STANDARD.md "Our
   own editor"). Grade 5 needs from it: variables with set, change and compare blocks; an event block that fires when a
   value is reached; a temporary "show value" or sound block for debugging; comments shown in the text view; and a way
   to paste one saved program's blocks into another (the remix and the team assembly), all in one file with no
   account. A separate small model-training tool with a drawing pad and on-device training is also asked (unit 4);
   until it exists, E5-ALG-ML-02 is met only on the paper path, marked part met. Whether Curio Class builds a training
   tool is a scope call (Cory's); my pick is yes, small and on-device, since grades 4, 5 and 6 rows all lean on one.
4. **Source list.** docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, beside the pilot's entries: the archived key stage 2 Year 6 unit pages
   (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2), the National Association of the Deaf's captioning pages, the
   W3C Internationalization article "Personal names around the world", and the US Bureau of Labor Statistics
   Occupational Outlook Handbook (bls.gov/ooh/) as the dated labor source for unit 5 session 2.
5. **Row changes** (notes and words cells; the lead applies them, listed in the final message): g5-professionals
   gains the boundary's cross-field professionals sharing work through digital tools; g5-variability drops "average"
   for "typical" and says no statistic is computed; g5-collect-tools names numeric and descriptive columns and the
   sort and group in the tool; g5-train-model draws on a drawing pad (no camera or microphone) and adds the look at
   what is missing from the training data; g5-human-centered adds the designer's values and accessibility and
   fairness considerations, and its "human-centred" becomes "human-centered"; g5-variability's "analysed" becomes
   "analyzed".

## Source map

The partial base for grade 5 is the archived edition of England's Teach Computing Curriculum, Year 6 (six units;
raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2, resources dated 2023). Placed from the public unit pages (lesson
titles and unit overviews), opened 2026-11-25; each archived Year 6 unit lists six lessons. The publisher teaches each
unit's lessons in number order; where this frame splits a unit or takes part of it, the row says why. Each adapted
lesson keeps its idea, is rewritten in our words for an American grade 5, replaces the named block language,
spreadsheet and board with our own editor, a table tool and paper, and is credited once a day. Check days are ours,
never a kept lesson.

| Base unit (Year 6) | Lessons | Placed in | How it is used | Left out, and why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Computing systems and networks: Communication and collaboration | 3 Working together; 4 Shared working; 5 How we communicate; 6 Communicating responsibly | Unit 5, sessions 2 and 4; unit 1, session 3 | Lessons 3 and 4: people working together through shared digital tools (unit 5 session 4); lesson 5: how computing changed the ways people communicate (unit 5 session 2); lesson 6: what to keep private, as the confidentiality side (unit 1 session 3), moved ahead because the triangle is unit 1's | None of 3 to 6 |
| Computing systems and networks: Communication and collaboration | 1 Internet addresses; 2 Data packets | None | | Both left out: addresses and packets are past the E5-SYS-NT-14 boundary and are grade 6's g6-packets (MS-SYS-NT-34); offered to the grade 6 frame |
| Programming A: Variables in games | 1 Introducing variables; 2 Variables in programming; 3 Improving a game; 5 Design to code; 6 Improving and sharing | Unit 3, sessions 1, 2, 5, 6 and 7 | Lessons 1 and 2: variables set and changed, traced first (sessions 1 and 2); lesson 3: improving a given game, as the remix (session 5); lessons 5 and 6: the designed game built and shared, as the team project's components, assembly and test (sessions 6 and 7). Sessions 3 and 4 (debugging strategies) are our own | None left out of these five; the named block language is replaced by our editor |
| Programming A: Variables in games | 4 Designing a game | Unit 2, session 2 | The game design sheet becomes the flowchart students draw, moved ahead of the code because the flowchart is unit 3's plan | None |
| Data and information: Introduction to spreadsheets | 1 Collecting data; 2 Formatting a spreadsheet | Unit 4, session 1 | Collecting data in a tool and organizing it by type in a table, sorted and grouped | None of 1 and 2 |
| Data and information: Introduction to spreadsheets | 3 What's the formula?; 4 Calculate and duplicate; 5 Event planning; 6 Presenting data | None | | All four left out: formulas, cost planning and charts go past E5-DAT-DC-09's boundary (no analysis or visualization asked) and use money and math this course does not teach; outside bar C1's 10 percent if kept |
| Programming B: Sensing movement | 5 Designing a step counter | Unit 2, session 1 | The step counter's rules as the anchor flowchart, on paper | Lessons 1 to 4 and 6 (the board, its inputs and making the counter): need a microcontroller board, optional in STANDARD.md section 5; left out, with the board named for a later optional unit |
| Creating media: Web page creation | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no E5 id at this depth, depends on a web-page tool |
| Creating media: 3D modelling | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no CSTA id, depends on a modeling app |

Units 1 (sessions 1 and 2), 2 (session 3), 3 (sessions 3 and 4), 4 (sessions 2 to 5) and 5 (sessions 1 and 3) are
our own: the partial base has no four-part system role-play, no network-parts unit at this age, no confidentiality,
integrity and availability, no human-centered design, no systematic debugging lesson, no variability, no machine
learning and no inclusion, emerging-technology or use-or-not cases.


