| C15 | **Screens serve the learning:** tied to the objective, run on school devices, usable without mouse or colour, private, with a non-screen path | The tool's named job in the section plan; the access check; single-file offline tools that store nothing off the device | 6, 7 | A tool has no named job. It fails keyboard-only or colour-free play. It needs an install, an account or a network. It stores data off the device |
| C16 | **Honest about evidence** | A one-page logic model per course; the plain statement that the course has not been tried with children | 1, 3 | Any claim of effect. No logic model. Research cited that nobody on the project read |

**Where our promises cost us marks, by choice.** Curio Class will not build some things rubrics ask for
(FOUNDATION, section 3): data dashboards (Texas names one as an example), adapting to each student and plugging
into a learning management system (Ohio, narrative items), accessible-format files lodged with a national centre
(Oregon), and professional learning tied to the materials (Ohio, scored). Our answers are the class record sheet,
score codes, open pages, print and teacher pages. Professional learning: none for now (decided 2026-11-11). It is
a future area of growth, not a closed door.

**Where the instruments conflict on values.** Oklahoma's arts rubric checks materials against state law. Maryland,
the federal health tool and the SEL registry ask for equity content in terms that pull the other way. No stance
passes all of them. C12 follows FOUNDATION promise 7, a middle line, and Curio Class does not claim to satisfy any
state's values criteria. Said once here, so it is not re-opened per course. Cory's values rule (2026-11-12) now
settles these conflicts in order: the higher court, then the evidence, then dignity and openness, then the age of
the student (see "Still open with Cory", b, and social studies item 11). FOUNDATION's promises still bind.

**Acceptability is local.** A national site cannot settle what suits each community, so each course page says what
a school may leave out. For health this is decided: all sensitive topics are carried, and each sensitive unit can
# Approach-to-teaching sources: access audit

Audit date: 2026-11-11. Purpose: confirm that every source the Curio Class "approach to teaching" document would cite can actually be obtained and read.

## How this was tested

- Every URL below was requested live (curl with a browser user agent) and the HTTP status and content type recorded. Nothing here relies on memory for reachability.
- HTML pages were then read with WebFetch to confirm real content came back.
- **Every PDF tested returns binary through WebFetch** (12 of 12 tried, plus one over the 10 MB WebFetch size cap). This is a tool limit, not a problem with the files. Each PDF was then streamed into PyMuPDF in memory to confirm a real text layer; page and word counts are given. Local tools that work on this PC: `pdftotext` (Git Bash, file input only, not stdin) and Python `pymupdf` / `pypdf`.
- Side effect to know about: WebFetch saves a copy of each binary PDF it fetches into the session `tool-results` folder, so those copies can be text-extracted directly.
- Sites that refuse automated clients: Sage Journals and Taylor and Francis (403 to curl and WebFetch), web.archive.org (429 to curl, refused by WebFetch), NCTM and the EEF toolkit page (WebFetch refused, curl fine), PMC (bot check for WebFetch).

Access codes used in the tables:

- **HTML full**: full text read as HTML through WebFetch.
- **HTML part**: page read, but only part of the source is on it.
- **PDF, extracts**: reachable; WebFetch gives binary; text layer confirmed (pages / words). Needs local download and text extraction to read.
- **PDF, scan**: reachable but image only, needs OCR.
- **Landing only**, **Blocked**, **Paywalled**, **Not found**: as named.

